.TH FBMQ-WORKER 1 "February 2026" "fbmq 1.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
fbmq-worker \- poll an fbmq queue and run a command for each task
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fbmq-worker
.RB [ \-p
.IR secs ]
.RB [ \-n
.IR count ]
.RB [ \-1 ]
.RB [ \-v ]
.I queue-dir
.I command
.RI [ args... ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B fbmq-worker
polls an
.BR fbmq (1)
queue directory for pending tasks. When a task is available, it pops the
message, pipes its body to the given command's stdin, and acks or nacks
based on the command's exit status.
.PP
Exit 0 from the command triggers an ack (message moves to done/).
Any non-zero exit triggers a nack (message returns to pending/ for retry,
or moves to failed/ after max retries).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-p " secs"
Poll interval in seconds when the queue is empty. Default: 5.
.TP
.BI \-n " count"
Exit after processing
.I count
tasks. Default: 0 (unlimited).
.TP
.B \-1
Process one task and exit. Alias for
.BR "\-n 1" .
.TP
.B \-v
Verbose logging to stderr. Logs poll, claim, ack, and nack events.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
The following environment variables are set for the command:
.TP
.B FBMQ_TASK_PATH
Full path to the claimed message file in processing/.
.TP
.B FBMQ_QUEUE
The queue directory.
.PP
The worker itself uses:
.TP
.B FBMQ
Path to the fbmq binary. Defaults to
.BR fbmq (1)
found in PATH.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
# Process build tasks
fbmq-worker /var/queue/builds ./handle-build.sh

# One-shot with verbose output
fbmq-worker -1 -v /var/queue/jobs my-handler

# Custom poll interval, pass args to command
fbmq-worker -p 10 /var/queue/deploys deploy.sh --env production

# Process 5 tasks and exit
fbmq-worker -n 5 /var/queue/jobs process-task
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Normal exit (limit reached or signal received).
.TP
.B 1
Missing arguments or fbmq not found.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fbmq (1),
.BR fbmq-message (5),
.BR fbmq-design (7)
